Abstarction
What is Abstract in Java?

Abstract means something that is incomplete or not fully defined.

In Java, abstraction is used to hide implementation details and show only the important features.
Why do we use Abstract?

We use abstract when:

We want to define a common rule for all subclasses
We want to force child classes to implement certain methods
We want to hide implementation details
We want code reusability and structure
Example of Abstract Class
“Some work done + some rules”

Abstract teacher:
Gives notes (ready content)
Also gives assignment (rule)
Interface teacher:

Only gives instruction:

“Write exam”

No notes, no explanation

abstract class Animal {

    // abstract method (no body)
    abstract void sound();

    // normal method (with body)
    void eat() {
        System.out.println("Animal is eating");
    }
}

// child class
class Dog extends Animal {

    // giving implementation for abstract method
    void sound() {
        System.out.println("Dog barks");
    }
}

// main class
public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {

        Dog d = new Dog();
        d.sound();   // Dog barks
        d.eat();     // Animal is eating
    }
}
output
Dog barks
Animal is eating


